# Trivy baseline for eg-esign — reviewed 2026-07-27.
#
# Only findings with no trivial fix belong here. Anything not listed still
# fails the build, so new vulnerabilities are caught.
#
# Fixed rather than ignored in the same change (16 -> 5): axios ^1.18.0,
# fast-uri ^3.1.4, brace-expansion ^5.0.8, @opentelemetry/propagator-jaeger
# ^2.9.0, next 16.2.11, sharp ^0.35.0.
#
# Verified against the built image: react-router, sharp and brace-expansion do
# ship in the runtime image, so these are real exposures, not dev-tree noise.
# Re-triage when the nightly upstream-sync bumps these.
#
# 2026-09-28: next 16.3.6 (postcss 8.5.23, sharp ^0.35.3), sharp 0.35.5 and
# brace-expansion ^5.0.9 cleared the postcss, sharp and brace-expansion
# entries that used to be here.

# react-router 7.18.1 -> 8.3.0. A 7->8 major of the app's own framework
# (apps/remix runs React Router in framework mode) — its own project, not a
# CI-unblocking patch.
GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2

# 2026-10-29: the lockfile updates, axios ^1.20.0 and next 16.4.1 cleared the
# other 23 findings. These two are fixed only in nodemailer 10 (10.0.5, 10.0.6;
# no 9.x backport). That is a major of the mail library every outgoing email
# goes through (packages/email), and @documenso/nodemailer-resend 5.0.0 still
# peers on nodemailer ^9, so it is its own change, not a CI-unblocking patch.
# Both are CPU denial of service in nodemailer's addressparser. Here it only
# parses addresses passed as plain strings (reply-to, members' account emails,
# fixed system addresses); each is a constant or validated by zEmail / zod
# .email() first, which rejects the parentheses, brackets and whitespace both
# exploits need. Recipient names, including ones typed by unauthenticated
# direct-template signers and at sign-up, are passed as { name, address }
# objects, which nodemailer does not run through addressparser. Keep it that
# way; remove these two lines with the nodemailer 10 upgrade.
CVE-2026-90776
GHSA-v53p-9fqp-m79j
